        {
          "uid": "rs-realm-event-backlog",
          "orgID": 1,
          "folderUID": "defd2d156sav4d",
          "ruleGroup": "realm-server-availability-group",
          "title": "Realm Server Realm Event Backlog",
          "condition": "B",
          "annotations": {
            "summary": "{{ if $values }}A realm-server process's realm event queue held an event for more than 60s before it started sending in every 1-minute bin of the last 5 minutes (the rule reduces the window with min, so one bin under the threshold keeps it quiet). Sourced from realmEventQueueAgeMs on the periodic health line: the wait of the oldest queued event, which a queue that drains keeps near zero however deep a burst is. A held backlog means realm events (index, update) reach browsers late, so cards and searches look stale. Read realmEventBackPressure on the same line: nonzero means synapse is overloaded and the queue is pacing itself to it, so look at synapse CPU and what is writing in bulk; zero means the queue is at its configured send rate (REALM_EVENT_SENDS_PER_SECOND, REALM_EVENT_MAX_SENDS_IN_FLIGHT). realmEventsDropped counts events discarded once the queue reached REALM_EVENT_MAX_QUEUED_BYTES.{{ else }}Grafana could not run this rule's query. This is the rule's DatasourceError alert, and its Error annotation gives the reason. It shows nothing about the realm event queue. The query reads CloudWatch: the Error annotation's text says why, usually throttling or a missing permission.{{ end }}"
          },
