        {
          "uid": "rs-db-pool-stuck",
          "orgID": 1,
          "folderUID": "defd2d156sav4d",
          "ruleGroup": "realm-server-availability-group",
          "title": "Realm Server DB Pool Stuck",
          "condition": "B",
          "annotations": {
            "summary": "{{ if $values }}A realm-server process had a database acquisition waiting more than 60s for a pool connection in every 1-minute bin of the last 5 minutes (the rule reduces the window with min, so one bin under the threshold keeps it quiet). Sourced from dbOldestWaitMs on the periodic health line: the wait of the oldest acquisition still queued. A pool that is only busy keeps its queue moving, so this stays short however many acquisitions wait (dbWaiting); it climbs when the pool's connections are held by work that is not finishing, and every database-touching request on that process hangs behind it. The load balancer's check and /_liveness do not touch the pool and stay green. Read dbIdleInTxMaxMs and dbAdvisoryWaitMaxMs on the same line: a long idle transaction is a holder that stopped driving its work, a long advisory wait is a writer queued behind a lock holder. pg_stat_activity names the sessions. The value is the highest across tasks; to find the task, run filter @message like /dbOldestWaitMs=/ | parse @message /dbOldestWaitMs=(?<v>\\d+)/ | stats max(v) by @logStream, bin(1m) in Logs Insights. Replacing that task drops its connections and clears a wedge it cannot leave on its own.{{ else }}Grafana could not run this rule's query. This is the rule's DatasourceError alert, and its Error annotation gives the reason. It shows nothing about the database pool. The query reads CloudWatch: the Error annotation's text says why, usually throttling or a missing permission.{{ end }}"
          },
          "data": [
            {
              "refId": "A",
              "queryType": "",
              "relativeTimeRange": {
                "from": 300,
                "to": 0
              },
              "datasourceUid": "cef5x9o3yzawwf",
              "model": {
                "datasource": {
                  "type": "cloudwatch",
                  "uid": "cef5x9o3yzawwf"
