          "uid": "rs-db-idle-in-transaction",
          "orgID": 1,
          "folderUID": "defd2d156sav4d",
          "ruleGroup": "realm-server-availability-group",
          "title": "Realm Server DB Transaction Left Idle",
          "condition": "B",
          "annotations": {
            "summary": "{{ if $values }}A database session of the realm-server's role sat idle inside an open transaction for more than 10 minutes at some point in the last 5 minutes. Sourced from dbIdleInTxMaxMs on the periodic health line, which a realm-server process reads from pg_stat_activity on a connection of its own every 30s; the reading covers the whole database, so every replica reports the same session. A request's transaction is idle between its statements for milliseconds. Publishing a realm holds its lock transaction idle on purpose while it waits for the source realm's indexing and copies the realm, for up to about 7 minutes on production; the threshold sits above that. Past it, a client has most likely stopped driving its transaction, and its locks, advisory locks included, stay taken until it ends. Find it in pg_stat_activity (state 'idle in transaction', oldest state_change) and read client_addr and query, its last statement, to see which task holds it and what it was doing.{{ else }}Grafana could not run this rule's query. This is the rule's DatasourceError alert, and its Error annotation gives the reason. It shows nothing about idle transactions. The query reads CloudWatch: the Error annotation's text says why, usually throttling or a missing permission.{{ end }}"
          },
